; 47-default.kurt

; `default SORT` gives the new symbols of a file a sort: a symbol without a declared sort and
; without a sorted place gets it at its first appearance -- as if it were declared there. A file
; about numbers says `default real` (natural.kurt does), and then every new name is a number.

load natural
default real

const a
a = a                  ; `a` appears first: `real a ; sort by default`
a + 1 = 1 + a          ; a number, as the default said

; The line can say otherwise: `A` is equal to a set, so `A` is a set -- not a number equal to a set
; of numbers (the sort of the other side of `=`).
const A
use A = {n ∈ Nat | n > 2}      ; `set A ; sort inferred`
expect "TypeError" "`A` has the sort `set`"
    use A + 1 = 1 + A

; A binder of the file ranges over the default too: `∀ n` here is over the numbers, so it says
; nothing about other objects (for two sets of numbers, natural.kurt's "ext-real" closes that gap).
use ∀ n (n + 0 = n)

; A symbol that should be any object says so: `any x` (or `set x` for a set).
any x
use x ∈ A ⇒ x ∈ A

; `default` alone names the default in force. `default any` is the same as none. The default
; doesn't cross `load` (a loading file has its own), but the sorts it gave do.
default
